-- title:
--   Integrality over k[j] descends along a field embedding
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ and $L$ be fields and let $\sigma\colon k\to L$ be a ring homomorphism. Write `coeffMap` $\sigma$ for the induced ring homomorphism $k(\!(q)\!)\to L(\!(q)\!)$ on Laurent series (formal Hahn series over $\mathbb{Z}$ with well-ordered support) given by applying $\sigma$ to each coefficient. Let $j,x\in k(\!(q)\!)$. The hypothesis is that the image $\sigma(x)$ is integral over the $L$-subalgebra of $L(\!(q)\!)$ generated by the single element $\sigma(j)$, i.e. over $L[\sigma(j)]$; integrality is taken in Mathlib's sense, so there exists a monic polynomial with coefficients in that subalgebra vanishing at $\sigma(x)$. The conclusion is that $x$ is integral over the $k$-subalgebra of $k(\!(q)\!)$ generated by $j$, that is over $k[j]$. No algebraicity, separability or finiteness assumption on the extension $\sigma$ is imposed, and $\sigma$ is not assumed to be anything more than a homomorphism of fields (hence injective).
--
--   This is the descent step that transports integrality of $q$-expansions over a large coefficient field (for instance $\mathbb{C}$, where modular forms are constructed analytically) back to a small one (for instance $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, where the function field of the modular curve lives), along an arbitrary embedding. It is used in the construction of modular functions and their mod $p$ counterparts, for example by [`ModPForms.exists_isModPCuspFormFn_qexpOfWeight_eq_of_mem_modPCusp`](thm.html#ModPForms.exists_isModPCuspFormFn_qexpOfWeight_eq_of_mem_modPCusp) and [`ModularCurve.exists_fieldOver_lift_isIntegral_of_isIntegral`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_fieldOver_lift_isIntegral_of_isIntegral).
